  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=27{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-3x-1$ have multiplicity $1?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =27{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-3x-1 \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(3x+1)-(3x+1) \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-1)(3x+1) \\\\  & =(3x-1)(3x+1)(3x+1) \\\\  & =(3x-1)(3x+1{)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(3x+1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(3x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$",
